course: dictionary
lesson: 14165
title: Memory tricks: mnemonics
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mnemonic is a trick that helps you remember something. It can be a rhyme, a short sentence or a pattern. Mnemonics is also the word for the study and use of these memory tricks.
explain: Each word in a mnemonic can stand for something you need to remember. «Never Eat Shredded Wheat» helps you remember North, East, South and West, because the first letter of each word matches the first letter of each direction.
explain: Mnemonics work because a silly sentence or a catchy rhyme is easier to remember than a plain list. When you have a list to learn, make up a mnemonic for it.
fact: A mnemonic is a memory trick.
fact: A mnemonic can be a rhyme, a sentence or a pattern.
fact: «Never Eat Shredded Wheat» helps you remember the four compass directions: North, East, South and West.
fact: «Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain» helps you remember the colours of the rainbow.
fact: Mnemonics is also the study and use of memory tricks.
exercise: «Never Eat Shredded Wheat» helps you remember what? | *The four directions: North, East, South and West | The four seasons | The names of four animals
exercise: What is a mnemonic? | A kind of food | *A trick that helps you remember something | A very long book
exercise: Which of these is a mnemonic? | The lid of a jar | The number 7 times 8 | *«Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain»
test: What can a mnemonic be? | A colour only | A heavy stone | *A rhyme, a sentence or a pattern
test: Why does a mnemonic help you remember? | It stops you from learning | *A catchy sentence or rhyme is easier to remember than a plain list | It is a long list of hard facts
exam: Mnemonics is also the name for what? | *The study and use of memory tricks | A kind of sweet | A map of the world
